===Etymology===
{{coinage|zh|Lu Xun}} in his short story, “{{w|My Old Home}}”. According to him, the sound component, {{m|zh|查|tr=zhā}} was similar to how the countryfolks said the animal's name. 

Whilst most modern sources identify the animal as a badger or a badger-like animal, {{zh-ref|Yu (2016)}} proposes that the animal might actually have been a {{w|water deer}} - the word ultimately coming from {{m|zh|獐|tr={{IPAchar|[*tsɒ̃~tsɒŋ]}}}} in some variety of non-urban {{w|Shaoxing dialect|Shaoxing Wu}} (likely what would have been spoken by the real-life inspiration for the character {{m|zh||閏土|tr=Rùntǔ}}, who tells young {{w|Lu Xun}} about the animal). 

This would have later been corrupted to {{IPAchar|[*tsɑ]}} in {{w|Lu Xun}}'s own Urban {{w|Shaoxing dialect|Shaoxing Wu}}, and eventually likened to Standard Mandarin {{m|zh|查|tr=zhā}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{zh-pron
|m=zhā,chá
|m_note=zhā - pronunciation given by earlier dictionaries such as the 1954 Xinhua Dictionary, whilst later standards mainly prescribed chá through sound correspondence with the usual Standard Mandarin reading of {{m|zh|查|tr=chá}}
|cat=
}}
{{rfp|wuu|Shaoxing Wu: /dzo/, /tsɑ/, or /tsɒ̃/?}}

===Definitions===
{{head|zh|hanzi}}

# A species of unidentified [[wild]] [[animal]] mentioned in {{w|Lu Xun}}'s “{{w|My Old Home}}”, potentially a [[badger]] or [[water deer]].
#: {{zh-x|我 那時 並 不 知道 這 所謂 猹{zhā} 的 是 怎麼 一件 東西——[[便 是]] 現在 也 不 知道——只是 無端 地 覺得 狀 如 小狗 而 很 兇猛。|Back then I did not know what this so-called '''''zha''''' was—which I still do not know even now—but just thought that it had a shape like a little dog and was somehow ferocious for no reason.|ref='''1921''', {{w|Lu Xun}}, “My Old Home”}}
#: {{zh-x|走路 的 人 口渴 了 摘 一個 瓜 吃，我們 這裡 是 不算 偷 的。要 管 的 是 '''獾''' 豬、刺蝟、'''猹{zhā}'''。|We don't count it as stealing here if a passerby happened to be thirsty and picked a melon to eat. What we do have to mind are '''badgers''' or pigs, hedgehogs, '''''zha''''', that sort of thing.|ref='''1921''', {{w|Lu Xun}}, “My Old Home”}}
#: {{zh-x|'''「猹{zhā}」''' 字 是 我 據 鄉下人 所 說 的 聲音， 生造 出來 的， 讀 如 '''「查{zhā}」'''。 但 我 自己 也 不 知道 究竟 是 怎樣 的 動物， 因為 這 乃 是 @閏土 所 說， 別人 不 知 其 詳。現在 想 起來， 也許 是 獾 罷。|The character '''''zha''''' is one I made up ad hoc according to the countrypeople's pronunciation of the word, similar to {{m|zh||查|tr=zhā}}. But not even I myself quite understand what kind of an animal this might be, since it was just something that Runtu had referred to; any other person would not have known about it. Now that I think about it though, it might have been a badger.|ref='''1929''', {{w|Lu Xun}}, Letter to Shu Xincheng}}

===Usage notes===
Earlier dictionary attestations listed the character under the pronunciation {{m|cmn|zhā|sc=Latn}} - the earliest being the first edition of the {{w|Xinhua Dictionary}}, chief edited by the lexicographer and linguist Wei Jiangong ({{lw|zh|魏建功}}). 

However, the 1973 {{w|Xiandai Hanyu Cidian}} had changed the character's pronunciation to {{m|cmn|chá|sc=Latn}}, homophonic to the more common reading of {{m|zh|查|tr=chá}} in Standard Mandarin. The first ''Pronunciation Lookup Table for Putonghua Words with Alternate Pronunciations'' ({{lw|zh|普通話異讀詞審音表|《普通話異讀詞審音表》}}), published in 1985, has similarly prescribed {{m|cmn|chá|sc=Latn}} in favour of {{m|cmn|zhā|sc=Latn}}. Most later sources have adhered to this and given the character's pronunciation as {{m|cmn|chá|sc=Latn}} since then.

